  "narrative": "The new 12-claim panel gives one large new association and several mostly negative or imprecise context/mechanistic follow-ups. I interpreted estimates exactly as signed to the registered direction, so a positive estimate supports the claimed direction and a negative estimate contradicts it.\n\nH41 is the key new finding: PTEN loss versus STAT3 dependency, direction -1 (stronger STAT3 dependency), has signed mean_difference +0.267, 95% CI [0.215,0.319]. Because the raw contrast is negative under direction -1, PTEN-loss samples show markedly more negative STAT3 dependency than PTEN-intact samples. This effect is far larger than a 10%-scale threshold for STAT3 scores (the STAT3 mean is about -0.28, so a 10% absolute change is roughly 0.028) and is also large relative to the STAT3 score SD. The interval is well away from zero and includes only clinically meaningful values. I accept H41 as an association in this supplied dataset and request independent validation because it is a strong new association where validation could materially strengthen or constrain the conclusion. I do not interpret PTEN loss as causally inducing STAT3 dependency; co-occurring PI3K/pathway alterations, lineage context, or screen artifacts could contribute, so I registered PTEN-STAT3 context tests.\n\nH20, H21, and H32 reject the proposed modifiers of the accepted SMARCA4-loss effect on SMARCA2 dependency. H20 tested adenocarcinoma subtype interaction: signed estimate -0.028, CI [-0.076,0.020]. H32 tested TP53-loss co-loss interaction: signed estimate -0.015, CI [-0.064,0.035]. Both intervals are centered near or opposite the claimed direction and do not support a clinically meaningful positive interaction; they are tiny relative to the accepted SMARCA4 main effect of +0.784. H21 tested ARID1A loss as an independent SWI/SNF-complex predictor of SMARCA2 dependency: signed estimate -0.017, CI [-0.057,0.023]. It is near zero and does not support stronger SMARCA2 dependency under ARID1A loss. These results do not threaten H6; rather, they suggest that the SMARCA2 dependency association is specific to SMARCA4 loss in this dataset and not broadly explained by adenocarcinoma subtype, TP53 co-loss, or ARID1A loss.\n\nH34, H36, and H37 test whether the accepted MSI-high/WRN and KEAP1/NFE2L2 associations have useful STK11 or lineage context. H34 MSI-high adenocarcinoma interaction is signed -0.021, CI [-0.085,0.042]; H36 KEAP1-by-STK11 interaction is signed -0.026, CI [-0.079,0.026]; H37 STK11 loss main effect on NFE2L2 is signed -0.007, CI [-0.046,0.033]. All are small relative to their accepted main effects and do not provide evidence for the claimed context modifications. However, some intervals include modest positive effects, so I record them as unresolved but deferred rather than as fully closed nulls. The practical conclusion is that these context refinements are low priority compared with H41 and H39.\n\nH22, H23, H38, and H40 are imprecise and not strong enough to accept. H22 EGFR amplification in KRAS-wild-type samples is signed +0.016, CI [-0.014,0.046]. H23 KRAS mutation in TP53-wild-type samples is signed +0.017, CI [-0.003,0.036]. H38 BRCA2 loss versus DHX9 dependency is signed +0.012, CI [-0.020,0.044]. H40 RB1 loss versus KIF18A dependency is signed +0.008, CI [-0.019,0.036]. These estimates are around or below a 10% absolute-scale threshold and have intervals including the null. I do not accept them; because their upper bounds include some clinically meaningful positive effects, I leave them unresolved but deferred unless future evidence or validation makes them informative.\n\nH39 RB1 loss versus POLR2A dependency is borderline: signed +0.025, CI [-0.0009,0.0507]. The point estimate is almost exactly at a roughly 10% absolute-scale threshold for POLR2A dependency, and the interval includes both the null and meaningfully larger positive effects. This is not enough to accept, but it is the most plausible remaining non-PTEN signal from this round, so I keep it active for possible validation or targeted follow-up. H40, using the same RB1 predictor but with KIF18A dependency, is much weaker and remains deferred.\n\nOverall, the strongest supported dataset findings after this round are H6 SMARCA4 loss associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency, H7 MSI-high associated with stronger WRN dependency, H13 KEAP1 loss associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, and now H41 PTEN loss associated with stronger STAT3 dependency. All are associations in the supplied dataset only. The requested validation for H41 will be assessed when it arrives.",
  "research_notes": "Round-2 appraise update. New strong accepted association: H41 PTEN loss -> stronger STAT3 dependency, signed +0.267 CI [0.215,0.319]; validation requested. Context tests around H6 mostly negative/near null: H20 adenocarcinoma interaction, H32 TP53-loss interaction, H21 ARID1A loss main effect all rejected for meaningful claimed direction, supporting specificity of SMARCA4 loss for SMARCA2 in this dataset. H34 MSI-high subtype interaction, H36 KEAP1-STK11 interaction, and H37 STK11 main effect are small and do not support claimed context effects, but remain unresolved/deferred because intervals include modest positive values. H22 EGFR in KRAS-WT, H23 KRAS in TP53-WT, H38 BRCA2-DHX9, and H40 RB1-KIF18A are unresolved/deferred due small estimates and intervals including null. H39 RB1 loss -> POLR2A is borderline: signed +0.025 CI [-0.0009,0.0507]; keep active as the next-best possible new effect after H41. Proposed next context tests: PTEN-STAT3 interactions by adenocarcinoma subtype and PIK3CA mutation, PTEN-STAT3 among PIK3CA-wild-type, and RB1-POLR2A subtype interaction. Do not infer causality or clinical generalizability; PTEN effect may reflect pathway co-alterations or other context.",
