  "narrative": "All newly delivered estimates were interpreted under the signed-estimate convention. For direction -1 claims, positive values mean stronger dependency or a stronger positive signed effect in the focal subgroup; for direction +1 claims, positive values mean weaker dependency or a stronger positive signed effect in the focal subgroup. For interaction claims, positive values mean the signed effect is larger in the high-Cas9 subgroup than in the complement.\n\nThe Cas9-activity interaction tests do not show a positive modification of the accepted effects. H88 SMARCA2 plus SMARCA4 loss interaction was small and positive, +0.014 [ -0.029, 0.058 ], so I leave it unresolved rather than accept it: it is not significantly positive, and the interval includes both modestly negative and modestly positive values. H89 MSI-high plus WRN interaction was negative, -0.039 [ -0.103, 0.025 ]; the upper bound is small relative to the very large accepted WRN main effect, so I reject this registered positive-interaction direction. H90 KEAP1 plus NFE2L2 interaction was tiny and positive, +0.005 [ -0.039, 0.050 ], and is unresolved. H91 PTEN plus STAT3 interaction was negative, -0.021 [ -0.132, 0.090 ]; because the upper bound could include a nontrivial positive modification, I leave it unresolved rather than reject it as precisely null. Overall, these results do not provide evidence that the accepted effects are stronger specifically in higher-Cas9 screens. They do not refute the accepted main effects because null or imprecise interaction estimates can occur when subgroup modification is small or absent; the main effects were accepted from discovery and independent validation or broad stratum replication, not from these interaction tests.\n\nThe mechanistic main-effect tests were mostly null or too small. H92 TP53 loss versus weaker RPL11 dependency gave a positive tiny estimate, +0.004 [ -0.016, 0.024 ]; I leave it unresolved because the interval is imprecise and includes possible modest effects, but the point estimate is not supportive. H93 BRCA2 loss versus stronger USP7 dependency gave +0.004 [ -0.064, 0.072 ]; this is unresolved because of imprecision, but there is no evidence supporting a meaningful positive association. H96 APC mutation versus stronger TMED10 dependency was +0.004 [ -0.020, 0.027 ], unresolved. H98 BRAF V600E versus weaker RIT1 dependency was +0.004 [ -0.025, 0.033 ], unresolved. These leave the biological interpretation uncertain but do not establish new associations.\n\nSeveral tests point opposite to the registered direction and have intervals that do not leave room for a scientifically meaningful positive effect. H94 MYC amplification versus weaker KRAS dependency gave -0.010 [ -0.032, 0.012 ]; the upper bound is small relative to the KRAS score SD, so I reject the positive direction. H95 PIK3CA mutation versus weaker ERBB2 dependency gave -0.004 [ -0.024, 0.017 ]; this is too small to support the claimed weaker ERBB2 dependency. H97 CDKN2A loss versus stronger KIF18A dependency gave -0.013 [ -0.033, 0.006 ], a small negative estimate; reject. H99 KRAS mutation versus weaker EGFR dependency gave -0.010 [ -0.027, 0.008 ]; reject. These null or opposite results argue against the proposed upstream/downstream bypass or mitotic-stress associations in this dataset, though they do not imply those biology is impossible generally.\n\nBecause no newly tested claim is strong enough to accept, I request independent validation only for H83. H83 is an accepted but small-cell stratum-specific robustness result: MSI-high versus stronger WRN dependency among TP53-loss lines, +0.833 [0.765,0.900] with only 24 exposed lines. Validating H83 is useful because it could confirm a large stratum effect or reveal that the accepted WRN association is unstable in that small TP53-loss subset. This does not affect the main validated MSI-high WRN effect, H7, but it would clarify whether the stratum-specific acceptance was driven by imprecise small-sample estimation.",
