  "narrative": "All estimates are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction. For direction -1 main effects, a positive signed estimate means stronger dependency; for direction +1 main effects, a positive signed estimate means weaker dependency. For interactions, a positive signed estimate means the signed main effect is larger in the focal subgroup than in the complement. I assessed all twelve newly delivered discovery results under this convention.\n\nThe targeted mechanistic results did not establish new associations. H86 tested STK11 loss within KRAS-mutant lines for stronger KRAS dependency: +0.014 [-0.026, 0.053]. The point estimate is positive but very small and imprecise; it does not support a clinically or biologically meaningful stronger KRAS dependency. Because the upper bound still permits a modest positive effect, I leave it unresolved but defer further work. H100 tested PTEN loss for weaker BRAF dependency: -0.023 [-0.046, 0.000]. This is opposite to the registered weaker-dependency direction and the interval barely allows zero, so I reject it. H101 tested BRCA2 loss for stronger POLR2A dependency: +0.013 [-0.019, 0.046]. This is again too small and imprecise to support a meaningful transcriptional- or repair-stress association, so it is unresolved and deferred rather than active.\n\nThe context-modification tests did not show meaningful amplification of the accepted core effects. H102 asked whether high mutation burden amplifies the MSI-high WRN dependency contrast: -0.025 [-0.091, 0.040]. The point estimate is negative and tiny relative to the main MSI-high effect of about +0.79; even positive values in the interval represent only a small fraction of the main effect. I reject this specific positive-interaction claim and note that the data do not support a simple generic mutation-burden continuum for WRN dependency. H103 tested high serum media as a modifier of PTEN loss STAT3 dependency: +0.047 [-0.057, 0.151]. The point is positive but the interval is wide and includes both negligible and potentially meaningful modification; this is unresolved and deferred because the main PTEN STAT3 association remains well supported, but serum modification is not established. H104 tested low 9p21 copy number as a modifier of SMARCA4 loss SMARCA2 dependency: +0.020 [-0.023, 0.063]. This is very small relative to the huge SMARCA4 main effect, so I reject it as evidence of meaningful context modification. H105 tested high research_signature_a as a modifier of KEAP1 loss NFE2L2 dependency: -0.027 [-0.088, 0.034]. This provides no evidence of positive modification and is rejected.\n\nH106 is the only near-positive new mechanistic result: NF1 loss for weaker BRAF dependency gave +0.021 [-0.005, 0.047]. The point estimate is in the hypothesized direction, but it is small, the interval crosses zero, and the upper bound is not large enough to justify acceptance or validation as a meaningful dataset association. I leave it unresolved and deferred. H107 tested MTAP loss for stronger POLR2A dependency: +0.005 [-0.015, 0.026]. This is essentially null and does not support the transcription/replication stress hypothesis; I reject and close it.\n\nThe constructed marker screens were uninformative, as expected for assays with no assigned biological role. H108 marker D SMARCA2: +0.002 [-0.030, 0.035]; H109 marker E WRN: -0.013 [-0.036, 0.011]; H110 marker F STAT3: +0.006 [-0.015, 0.028]. All are close to zero or opposite to the registered direction, so I reject and close them. These nulls do not imply biological irrelevance generally, only that these constructed markers are not useful predictors for these dependency scores in this dataset.\n\nI do not request independent validation. None of the new claims has a large, precise, hypothesized-direction effect; validating a small near-zero result would be unlikely to change the substantive conclusion. The accepted core associations remain SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency,