  "narrative": "All estimates are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction. For direction +1 main effects, a positive signed estimate means the exposed group has a less negative dependency score and therefore weaker dependency. H13 tested KEAP1 loss against weaker NFE2L2 dependency. Discovery evidence R8 gave signed estimate +0.796 [0.776, 0.816]. The newly due independent validation evidence R66 gave signed estimate +0.791 [0.763, 0.819]. Both intervals are entirely positive and far from zero. The magnitude is very large relative to the NFE2L2 dependency score distribution: standard deviation about 0.363, so the difference is roughly 2.2 standard deviations. This is not a marginal or precision-dependent result; it is a large and precisely estimated dataset association. I therefore accept H13 and close the investigation. This establishes that, within the supplied lung CRISPR dataset, KEAP1-loss lines show less negative dependency_NFE2L2 scores, consistent with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, but it remains an association rather than a causal or generalizable biological claim.\n\nThis reinforces the current main scientific pattern. Four associations are now strongly supported by discovery plus independent validation or broad replication: SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency, MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency, KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, and PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency. The effect sizes for SMARCA4-SMARCA2, MSI-WRN, and KEAP1-NFE2L2 are each on the order of about 0.8 dependency-score units; the PTEN-STAT3 association is smaller, about 0.27 units, but still consistently positive, precisely estimated, and replicated across strata and independent validation. These remain dataset-specific associations, not proof of causal synthetic lethality or clinically actionable generalization.\n\nThe negative and unresolved evidence is also scientifically useful. Several intuitive oncogene-pathway dependencies are not supported as measured predictor associations in this dataset: EGFR amplification with stronger EGFR dependency, ERBB2 amplification with stronger ERBB2 dependency, BRAF V600E with stronger BRAF dependency, KRAS mutation with stronger KRAS dependency, KRAS mutation with weaker EGFR dependency, PIK3CA mutation with weaker ERBB2 or KRAS dependency, MYC amplification across DHX9/POLR2A/RPL11/KIF18A/MDM2/USP7, RB1 loss with POLR2A or KIF18A dependency, TP53 loss with RPL11 or MDM2 dependency, CDKN2A loss with KIF18A dependency, ARID1A loss with SMARCA2 dependency, NF1 loss with stronger KRAS dependency, and the newly tested MTAP loss with POLR2A dependency. Some near-mechanistic hypotheses remain unresolved because estimates are small or intervals imprecise, including STK11 loss within KRAS-mutant lines for KRAS dependency, BRCA2 loss for POLR2A dependency, NF1 loss for weaker BRAF dependency, and serum modification of the PTEN-STAT3 association. Constructed research markers and signature-interaction screens did not produce useful large effects, as expected for artificial assays with no assigned pathway role.\n\nContext and specificity tests so far mostly support robustness of the core associations rather than strong modification. High mutation burden does not appear to amplify the MSI-high WRN effect, arguing against a simple generic mutation-burden continuum for this dataset. Low 9p21 copy number does not meaningfully modify SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2 dependency. Research signature A does not meaningfully modify KEAP1-loss NFE2L2 dependency. Cas9-activity interaction tests do not show evidence that the accepted effects are stronger in higher-Cas9 screens, although some interaction estimates remain imprecise. The serum modification test for PTEN-STAT3 remains uncertain and should be interpreted only if the registered interaction analysis is run and gives a large precise estimate.\n\nI defer H85 and H87 as investigation-only bookkeeping because they extend KRAS and ERBB2 bypass logic that has been weakly supported or contradicted elsewhere in the run, and because the currently active culture-type robustness tests and the new PTEN-loss ERBB2 dependency test are more informative next. No independent validation request is needed now because H13 validation was already delivered and accepted, while no newly discovered result has a sufficiently large and precise hypothesized-direction effect to justify a new validation.\n\nThe most informative next analyses are the newly registered culture-context interactions for the four core accepted effects plus the focused mechanistic test of PTEN loss against weaker ERBB2 dependency. Culture-type interactions are useful robustness tests: a large positive interaction would indicate that adherent versus non-adherent context substantially modifies the association, whereas small or null interactions would support that the accepted effects are not culture-state artifacts. If PTEN loss shows a large, precise association with ERBB2 dependency in the registered direction, that would be a new candidate signal worth validation; if null, it would close a PI3K/PTEN bypass hypothesis for ERBB2 dependency in this dataset.",
  "research_notes": "Round-7 synthesis update. Signed convention: direction -1 positive = stronger dependency; direction +1 positive = weaker dependency; interaction positive = larger signed effect in focal subgroup. H13 reassessed and reaffirmed accepted: KEAP1 loss associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency. Discovery R8 +0.796 [0.776,0.816]; validation R66 +0.791 [0.763,0.819]. Huge effect about 2.2 SD of dependency_NFE2L2, very robust. Core accepted validated/replicated associations: SMARCA4 loss stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high stronger WRN dependency; KEAP1 loss weaker NFE2L2 dependency; PTEN loss stronger STAT3 dependency. Negative/unsupported: EGFR/EGFR, ERBB2/ERBB2, BRAF/BRAF, KRAS/KRAS, KRAS-EGFR, PIK3CA-ERBB2/KRAS, MYC-DHX9/POLR2A/RPL11/KIF18A/MDM2/USP7, RB1-POLR2A/KIF18A, TP53-RPL11/MDM2, CDKN2A-KIF18A, ARID1A-SMARCA2, NF1-KRAS, MTAP-POLR2A, marker screens. Some unresolved small/uncertain: STK11/KRAS KRAS-mutant, BRCA2/POLR2A, NF1/BRAF, serum x PTEN/STAT3. Context tests mostly null: high TMB x MSI/WRN rejected; low 9p21 x SMARCA4/SMARCA2 rejected; signature A x KEAP1/NFE2L2 rejected; Cas9 interactions no clear positive. Defer H85/H87. Next prioritize H111-H115 culture_type interactions for SMARCA2/WRN/NFE2L2/STAT3 and H115 PTEN loss vs ERBB2. No new validation; validation budget 4 new requests remain. All claims are dataset associations, not causality or generalizable clinical effects."
