  "narrative": "All delivered estimates are interpreted as already signed to the registered hypothesis direction. For direction -1 main effects, a positive signed estimate means stronger dependency; for direction +1 main effects, a positive signed estimate means weaker dependency. For interaction claims, a positive signed estimate means the signed main effect is larger in the focal subgroup than in its complement. These are associations in the supplied dataset only, not causal or generalizable claims.\n\nThe culture-type interaction tests largely support robustness of the accepted core effects, with one possible exception. H111 tested whether adherent culture amplifies SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2 dependency and gave +0.0145 [-0.0289, 0.0580]. This is tiny relative to the accepted SMARCA4 main effect of about +0.78 to +0.80, and the interval includes negative, zero, and only modest positive modification. I reject this specific positive-interaction claim and close it; culture type does not appear to meaningfully modify SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2 dependency in this dataset. H113 tested whether adherent culture amplifies the KEAP1-loss weaker NFE2L2 dependency effect and gave -0.0187 [-0.0643, 0.0269]. The point is small and negative, and the interval excludes any nontrivial positive modification relative to the accepted KEAP1 main effect of about +0.79 to +0.81. I reject and close it. H114 tested whether adherent culture amplifies PTEN-loss STAT3 dependency and gave -0.0228 [-0.1493, 0.1038]. The point estimate is negative and imprecise; the upper bound allows a potentially meaningful positive culture interaction, but the evidence is not strong enough to accept. I leave it unresolved and keep the investigation active because I registered a direct adherent-culture follow-up.\n\nH112 is the one newly delivered culture interaction that is precise and in the hypothesized direction. It tested whether adherent culture amplifies the MSI-high stronger WRN dependency effect and gave +0.1046 [0.0370, 0.1722]. This means the signed MSI-high WRN effect is larger in adherent lines than in non-adherent lines. Relative to the accepted overall MSI-high WRN main effect of about +0.79 to +0.81, this is roughly a 13% modification of the main effect, and it is about 0.40 SD of the WRN dependency distribution. The lower bound is clearly positive, so I accept this interaction as a discovery-level association. However, the non-adherent exposed cell contains only 29 lines, just above the minimum, and the interaction is a difference-in-differences quantity that could be sensitive to culture-type imbalance or confounding with MSI-high status or other predictors. Because this is a newly discovered, precise positive effect and not merely a tiny null, I request independent validation of H112. Acceptance now is discovery-level and remains dataset-specific; validation will be reassessed when it arrives.\n\nH115 tested PTEN loss for weaker ERBB2 dependency and gave +0.0018 [-0.0221, 0.0256], essentially null. This does not support a PTEN-loss bypass relationship with reduced ERBB2 dependency in this dataset, so I reject and close it. This is useful negative evidence because it closes a plausible PI3K/PTEN bypass hypothesis for ERBB2 dependency.\n\nThe cellular-state interactions were mostly null, except H116. H116 tested whether high EMT amplifies PTEN-loss stronger STAT3 dependency and gave +0.1175 [-0.0060, 0.2410]. The point estimate is large relative to the accepted PTEN STAT3 main effect of about +0.27, potentially a meaningful EMT-dependent modification, but the interval crosses zero and the lower bound is slightly negative. I do not accept it yet, but the magnitude and plausible biology justify keeping it active. I registered direct stratified mean-difference follow-ups in EMT-high and lower-or-equal EMT contexts to inspect whether PTEN-loss STAT3 dependency is materially stronger in the EMT-high group. H117 tested hypoxia amplification of the KEAP1-loss weaker NFE2L2 effect and gave -0.0225 [-0.0701, 0.0251]. This gives no evidence of positive modification relative to the large KEAP1 main effect; I reject and close it. H118 tested high MYC expression amplification of SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2 dependency and gave +0.0335 [-0.0096, 0.0766], a small modification relative to the huge SMARCA4 main effect; I reject and close it. H119 tested IFN-gamma high amplification of MSI-high WRN dependency and gave +0.0240 [-0.0357, 0.0838]. The point estimate is small, the interval is compatible with no meaningful interaction, and the upper bound is only a modest fraction of the MSI-high main effect; I reject and close it. Together, H113, H117, H118, and H119 support robustness of the core effects rather than broad cellular-state dependence.\n\nThe driver-context refinements did not rescue apparent oncogene-addiction nulls. H120 tested EGFR amplification against EGFR dependency in KRAS wild-type and PIK3CA wild-type lines and gave +0.0205 [-0.0112, 0.0521]. The point is in the stronger-dependency direction but very small and imprecise. Because the interval still permits a modest positive effect and because the claim is a cleaner-context refinement rather than an overall effect, I leave it unresolved and defer it, but it adds little support for EGFR dependency and is consistent with the broader EGFR amplification null. H121 tested ERBB2 amplification against ERBB2 dependency in the same clean driver context and gave -0.0182 [-0.0543, 0.0179], opposite to the registered stronger-dependency direction and clinically uninformative. I reject and close it. H122 tested ARID1A loss against SMARCA2 dependency within SMARCA4-loss lines and gave +0.0342 [-0.0131, 0.0815]. The point estimate is small relative to the dominant SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2 effect, though the upper bound permits a modest additional effect. I leave it unresolved and defer it; this does not establish a strong cumulative SWI/SNF subunit effect, but it does not entirely exclude a small incremental difference.\n\nA critical limitation is multiplicity: these are discovery 95% Welch intervals and many claims have been tested. Therefore I reserve acceptance for effects that are both directionally hypothesized and sizable on the relevant outcome scale or relative to the accepted main effect. The 10% relative-difference threshold is not naturally interpretable for negative dependency scores, so I also judge effects relative to outcome SD and to the magnitude of established core associations. H112 meets this bar at discovery level and is the only result justified for validation. H116 is the most important unresolved mechanistic hypothesis, and the registered stratified direct effects should clarify whether the borderline interaction reflects a real EMT modification or imprecision.",
